F. DIAGNOSIS - shadow.fsh:71 (0.30 + focus*1.5), quantified, decided NOT to change
==============================================================================
The caster tint line (pre-existing; NOT modified this round):
  program/shadow.fsh:71  shadowTint *= (0.30 + focus * 1.5) * (1.0 - a32);
Underwater it multiplies essentially the whole direct-sun term: the water caster is the
only translucent caster on the light path, so transparentWeight resolves to 1 and
diffuse.glsl:281-283 / shadowmap.glsl:152-154 pick caster.gba for isEyeInWater == 1.

Astra's caster multiplier  A = 0.30 + 1.5 * focus_a  (focus_a = solas_water_caustics)
Solas V2.2b's own mapping  S = 0.25 + 4.0 * caustics_s  (shadow.glsl:63; defaults
  WATER_CAUSTICS_STRENGTH=4.00, WATER_NORMAL_BUMP=0.4, WATER_NORMAL_OFFSET=0.5)
  ASTRA  caster multiplier p50/p95/max/mean = 0.450/1.313/2.667/0.581
  SOLAS  caster multiplier p50/p95/max/mean = 0.322/0.574/1.067/0.355
  -> Astra's term is BRIGHTER than its own reference (median 0.450 vs 0.322, mean 0.581 vs 0.355): it cannot explain 'Astra darker than the reference'.

Relative weight against the two fixes this round:
  * beams from always-zero : shaft term 0 -> composite gain 86.5x (volumetric only;
    the seabed direct term gains nothing from it).
  * caustic gain from a flat 0.44 floor : median seabed direct multiplier 0.44 -> 0.995
    (+126%), and it creates the bright spots (max 1.80).
  * caster term (this item)             : unchanged this round; median multiplier 0.45.
    Re-centring it to a median of 1.0 would be +122% - the same order as the caustic
    fix - but it is reference-derived and already more generous than the reference.

DECISION: do NOT change. Reason: (1) the coefficient is a deliberate 2026-09-11
calibration of Solas V2.2b's own '0.25 + caustics*STRENGTH' mapping to Astra's field
scale (documented in qa/session-log.md and cited as '保住海床底色' in
docs/2026-09-20-shader-pack-design-survey.md F-10); (2) measured against the reference
it is a brightening, not a dimming, so it is not the cause; (3) it scales ALL underwater
direct light and carries the water colour cast, so changing it is a global behaviour
change outside the three requirements and needs a visual A/B the round does not have.
If a later round still wants it lighter, the minimal form is to raise the floor only,
e.g. 0.30 -> 0.55 (median multiplier 0.45 -> 0.70) and/or clamp focus to [0,1] as the
tint's mix weight already does; both are single-constant edits in shadow.fsh.

